 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Training iterations are comparable to standard NeRF. On a single RTX A5000, a DS-NeRF training iteration takes ~362.4 ms, compared to ~359.8 ms for standard NeRF [S1].
-*   **Speedup:** DS-NeRF reaches a target test PSNR in 2-3x fewer training iterations than NeRF [S1]. In a 5-view setup, it achieves NeRF's peak test PSNR approximately 13 hours faster [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** No significant additional memory overhead compared to standard NeRF, as SfM keypoints are sparse and pre-computed [S1].
-*   **Scaling:** Performance gains are magnified at lower view counts. In a 5-view setup, DS-NeRF achieves NeRF's peak test PSNR ~13 hours faster [S1]. With 2 views, NeRF fails to match DS-NeRF's performance [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Accelerates training, improves quality with fewer views, requires no additional training data or domain-specific priors, compatible with other NeRF methods [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Relies on the quality of SfM; fails if SfM produces poor depth estimates. Assumes Gaussian uncertainty for keypoints [S1].
-*   **When to use:** Sparse-view scenarios, limited training time, scenes where SfM succeeds.
-*   **When not to use:** Scenes with poor SfM reconstruction, or when dense depth maps are available and can be used directly (though DS-NeRF can support other depth sources, its primary design is for SfM keypoints) [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Training iterations are comparable in wall-clock time to standard NeRF. On an RTX A5000, a DS-NeRF iteration takes ~362.4 ms vs ~359.8 ms for standard NeRF [S1].
+*   **Convergence Speed:** DS-NeRF reaches target test PSNR in 2-3x fewer training iterations than standard NeRF [S1]. In a 5-view setup, it achieves peak PSNR approximately 13 hours faster [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing sparse 3D keypoints and their uncertainties, which is negligible compared to the NeRF network weights [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Significantly reduces the number of input views required for high-quality synthesis (e.g., improving PSNR from 13.5 to 20.2 with very few views) [S1]. It is compatible with other NeRF methods (e.g., PixelNeRF, metaNeRF) and does not rely on domain-specific priors [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Performance is dependent on the quality of the SfM reconstruction. Poor depth estimates or inaccurate depth measurements can cause optimization failure [S1]. The method assumes Gaussian uncertainty for keypoints, which may not hold for non-SfM depth sources [S1]. Dense MVS depth is not used during training [S1].
 
 ## Background
-*   **Origin:** Introduced by [S1] (Chen et al., CVPR 2022).
-*   **History:** Developed to address the failure mode of NeRF fitting incorrect geometries with insufficient input views. It leverages the "free" depth supervision from SfM keypoints, which are already required for NeRF training [S1].
-*   **Usage:** Implemented for training on DTU, Redwood, and NeRF Real datasets with 2, 5, or 10 input views [S1]. Compatible with other NeRF methods like PixelNeRF and metaNeRF [S1].
+DS-NeRF was introduced to address the failure mode of NeRF when given insufficient input views, where the model fits incorrect geometries due to lack of constraints on empty space and opaque surfaces [S1]. The method leverages the "free" depth supervision provided by sparse 3D point clouds generated during standard SfM preprocessing (e.g., COLMAP) [S1]. It was demonstrated on datasets including DTU, Redwood, and NeRF Real with 2, 5, or 10 input views [S1]. The approach is distinct from methods using data-driven priors (like PixelNeRF) as it requires no additional training on other scenes and generalizes to any scene where SfM succeeds [S1].
 
 ## Sources
